WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 278: Jackie Robinson: Breaking the Color Barrier, Branch Rickey, and Number 42

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:19.600
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series honors the fearless athlete and civil rights pioneer who crossed the color line in America's national pastime and changed the conscience of a nation.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.160
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Jackie Robinson.

5
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:40.560
[George] Born in January 1919 in a sharecropper's wooden cabin in Cairo, Georgia, Jack Roosevelt Robinson was the youngest of five children of Mally Robinson, whose husband abandoned the family when Jackie was six months old.

6
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:55.440
[George] In 1920, Mally packed her children onto a Jim Crow railroad car and joined the Great Migration to Pasadena, California, purchasing a home on Pepper Street where neighbors circulated a petition demanding the black family leave.

7
00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:07.600
[George] Raised in poverty, Jackie discovered his peerless athletic gifts on the playgrounds of Southern California, attending Pasadena Junior College before enrolling at UCLA in 1939.

8
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:19.360
[George] At UCLA, Robinson became the first athlete in university history to win varsity letters in four distinct sports: football, basketball, track and field, and baseball.

9
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.360
[George] He led the Pacific Coast Conference in basketball scoring.

10
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:39.680
[George] Averaged 12 yards per carry as a dazzling collegiate football running back, won the NCAA Long Jump Championship with a leap of over 24 feet, and batted for the Bruin Baseball Squad, establishing himself as the greatest all-around athlete of his generation.

11
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:53.120
[George] His mother Mally worked 16-hour days scrubbing floors and washing laundry for affluent white families in Pasadena, teaching her children that self-respect and dignity were gifts that no white person could bestow or revoke.

12
00:01:53.120 --> 00:02:00.680
[George] At UCLA, Robinson was an electric athletic phenomenon whose prowess on the gridiron drew national acclaim.

13
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.360
[George] Playing alongside Kenny Washington and Woody Strode in the Bruins racially integrated backfield.

14
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.760
[George] He led the entire nation in punt return average with an astonishing 21 yards per return.

15
00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:27.400
[George] Despite his athletic celebrity, Jackie was barred from competing in social dances and restricted to segregated sections of local movie theaters, forging in him an unyielding determination to challenge racial caste wherever he encountered it.

16
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:43.160
[George] Drafted into the United States Army in 1942 following the outbreak of World War II, Robinson was initially denied admission to officer candidates school due to military racial quotas until heavyweight champion Joe Louis intervened on his behalf.

17
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:54.120
[George] Commissioned as a second lieutenant in the tank battalion at Fort Hood, Texas, Robinson's fierce commitment to human dignity was tested on July 6, 1944.

18
00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:03.800
[George] Boarding an unsegregated army bus with a fellow officer's light-skinned wife, the civilian bus driver snarled at Robinson to move to the back of the bus.

19
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:13.960
[George] Knowing that War Department regulations had recently desegregated military buses, Robinson flatly refused, stating, I'm not going to move a damn inch.

20
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:20.200
[George] Military police were summoned, and Robinson was subjected to racial insults by arresting officers.

21
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:33.800
[George] Charged with insubordination and conduct unbecoming an officer, Robinson demanded a full court martial, where his pristine service record and commanding testimony resulted in a unanimous acquittal and an honorable discharge.

22
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.840
[George] The racial climate at Fort Hood was venomous.

23
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:49.600
[George] Black soldiers in the 761st Black Panthers Tank Battalion were routinely subjected to humiliating abuse by local white civilians and military commanders who viewed black servicemen with visceral contempt.

24
00:03:49.600 --> 00:04:05.280
[George] When civilian bus driver Milton Renegar ordered Robinson to the back of the bus, Robinson stood his ground with absolute moral composure, pointing out that military desegregation orders signed by civilian authorities superseded local Jim Crow customs.

25
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:20.880
[George] At his court martial, Robinson's defense attorney skillfully dismantled the fabricated charges of disorderly conduct and disrespect, exposing the trial as a blatant attempt by white Southern officers to punish a proud black soldier who refused to bow his head.

26
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:37.360
[George] In 1945, Robinson joined the Kansas City monarchs of the Negro American League, earning $400 a month while enduring the grueling indignities of segregated hotels, endless bus travel, and exclusion from roadside diners across the South.

27
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:52.080
[George] Meanwhile, in Brooklyn, New York, Branch Ricky, the devout Methodist president and general manager of the Brooklyn Dodgers, was secretly preparing to shatter Major League Baseball's six-decade-old gentlemen's agreement barring black players.

28
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.040
[George] Ricky needed more than a superstar player.

29
00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:03.760
[George] He needed a man of immense personal character, intellect, and self-control who could carry the hopes of an entire race without cracking under pressure.

30
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:13.440
[George] On August 28, 1945, in Ricky's office at 215 Montague Street, the two men held a historic three-hour confrontation.

31
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:21.760
[George] Ricky hurled racist epithets, lunged with clenched fists, and simulated the insults Jackie would face from opposing players and fans.

32
00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.080
[George] When Robinson asked, Mr.

33
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.080
[George] Ricky, do you want a Negro who isn't afraid to fight back?

34
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.880
[George] Ricky leaned forward and roared, I want a ballplayer with guts enough not to fight back.

35
00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.320
[George] Traveling across the South with the Kansas City monarchs.

36
00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:48.440
[George] Robinson slept on buses parked at gas stations because white hotels turned black ballplayers away, fueling his intense disgust with the degrading conditions of the Negro leagues.

37
00:05:48.440 --> 00:06:00.360
[George] Branch Ricky, a devout moralist who saw baseball integration as both a moral imperative and a shrewd business move, spent months secretly scouting Robinson's temperament and mental fortitude.

38
00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:24.040
[George] During their epic August 1945 interview, Ricky leaned in so close their faces almost touched, warning Robinson that for three full years he would be unable to protest bad umpire calls, strike back at beanballs, or verbally respond to racist taunts from opposing dugouts, demanding that he conquer racial bigotry through the sheer unanswerable majesty of his play.

39
00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:43.640
[George] Assigned to the Dodgers' top minor league affiliate, the Montreal Royals of the International League, Robinson faced an immediate barrage of hostility during spring training in Florida, where municipal authorities locked stadium gates, padlocked ballparks, and cancelled games to prevent a black man from taking the field.

40
00:06:43.640 --> 00:07:01.080
[George] But when the season opened on April 18, 1946, against the Jersey City Giants at Roosevelt Stadium, Robinson electrified 25,000 spectators by collecting four hits, including a three run home run, scoring four runs, and stealing two bases.

41
00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:15.000
[George] In French speaking Montreal, the fans embraced Jackie and his beloved wife Rachel with open arms and passionate devotion, leading the Royals to the Little World Series Championship while leading the league with a.349 batting average.

42
00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:25.600
[George] Delirious Montreal fans chased Robinson down the street after the final game, prompting Pittsburgh courier journalist Sam Lacey to write the immortal line.

43
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:32.640
[George] It was probably the only day in history that a black man ran from a white mob with love instead of lynch on its mind.

44
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:46.080
[George] In Florida during spring training, local sheriffs threatened to halt exhibition games if Robinson took the field, forcing the Royals to stage clandestine intra-squad scrimmages behind locked doors in Daytona Beach.

45
00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:58.720
[George] In Montreal, however, French Canadian fans welcomed Robinson and his wife Rachel into their neighborhoods with open warmth, renting them comfortable apartments and bringing gifts of food and flowers to their doorstep.

46
00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.560
[George] Robinson responded by putting on an offensive masterclass.

47
00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:16.880
[George] Leading the International League in runs scored and fielding percentage as Montreal swept the Louisville Colonels in the Little World Series, proving beyond doubt that black ballplayers possessed the talent and temperament to dominate professional baseball.

48
00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:35.280
[George] On Tuesday, April 15, 1947, wearing the blue and white home uniform of the Brooklyn Dodgers with number 42 emblazoned across his back, 28-year-old Jackie Robinson trotted out to first base at Ebbett's Field before 26,000 fans, breaking the modern color line.

49
00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.120
[George] The ordeal that followed was an inferno of racial bigotry.

50
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:50.480
[George] Opposing managers, led by Ben Chapman of the Philadelphia Phillies, screamed vicious racial curses from the dugout, opposing pitchers threw fastballs aimed deliberately at his skull.

51
00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.920
[George] And runners intentionally sharpened their spikes to slice open his legs on tag plays.

52
00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:56.320
[George] St.

53
00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:04.600
[George] Louis Cardinals players threatened a league-wide strike until National League President Ford Frick threatened to suspend any striking player for life.

54
00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:13.640
[George] Robinson endured hate mail, death threats promising to shoot him on the field, and hotel bans that forced him to sleep apart from his teammates.

55
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:21.000
[George] Keeping his sacred promise to Ricky, Jackie never raised a fist, answering hate with unmatched athletic brilliance.

56
00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:29.240
[George] The vitriol Robinson endured during his 1947 rookie campaign was unprecedented in the annals of American professional sports.

57
00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:40.120
[George] Opposing players held black cats up in dugouts, threw watermelon rinds onto the field, and directed streams of vile racial obscenities at him throughout nine innings.

58
00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:41.720
[George] Dodgers' shortstop P.

59
00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:56.760
[George] Wee Reese, a white native of Kentucky, silenced a hostile crowd in Cincinnati by walking across the diamond, putting his arm around Robinson's shoulders, and chatting amiably before 40,000 spectators in an immortal gesture of solidarity.

60
00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:11.720
[George] Despite being hit by nine pitches and having his calves ripped open by the sharpened cleats of Eno's slaughter, Robinson refused to retaliate, leading the National League in stolen bases with 29 and guiding the Dodgers to the National League pennant.

61
00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:22.040
[George] Robinson revolutionized the strategic geometry of baseball, introducing the dynamic speed and daring base running of the Negro leagues into the conservative major leagues.

62
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:28.440
[George] He took enormous leads, danced off the bags to rattle opposing pitchers into bulks and wild pitches.

63
00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:40.840
[George] And executed the most thrilling play in sports, stealing home 19 times in his career, including his legendary steal of home against Yogi Berra in Game One of the 1955 World Series.

64
00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:46.480
[George] Robinson won the inaugural Major League Baseball Rookie of the Year Award in 1947.

65
00:10:44.760 --> 00:11:00.640
[George] Captured the National League Most Valuable Player Award in 1949 after leading the league with a.342 batting average and 37 stolen bases, and led the Boys of Summer Dodgers to six National League pennants.

66
00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:13.120
[George] In October 1955, Robinson helped Brooklyn finally conquer their perennial nemesis, the New York Yankees, delivering the first and only World Series championship in Brooklyn Dodgers history.

67
00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:26.160
[George] Robinson's dynamic style of play fundamentally dismantled the static, predictable offensive strategies of the 1940s, replacing conservative slugging with explosive tactical warfare on the basebaths.

68
00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:38.880
[George] Pitchers became so terrified of his dancing leads off third base that they threw wild pitches into the grandstands, while catchers were routinely caught out of position trying to anticipate his sudden sprints toward the plate.

69
00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:59.360
[George] His dramatic steal of home in the eighth inning of Game 1 of the 1955 World Series, sliding feet first beneath the lunging tag of catcher Yogi Berra as umpire Bill Summers signaled safe, ignited a fiery fury from Berra that lasted for decades, serving as the defining image of Robinson's peerless competitive fire.

70
00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:13.280
[George] Retiring from baseball in 1957, Robinson became an executive at Chalk Full O'Nuts, the first black vice president of a major American corporation, and helped found the black-owned Freedom National Bank in Harlem.

71
00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.280
[George] He worked closely with Dr.

72
00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:28.160
[George] Martin Luther King J.R., raised funds for the NAACP, and wrote syndicated newspaper columns demanding an end to racial segregation in housing, employment, and the corporate world.

73
00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:40.040
[George] Inducted into the national Baseball Hall of Fame in 1962 in his first year of eligibility, Robinson insisted on being judged solely on his statistics rather than his social impact.

74
00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:54.200
[George] In October 1972, nine days before his untimely death from diabetes and heart disease at age 53, Robinson stood on the field at Riverfront Stadium in Cincinnati, expressing his final public hope.

75
00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.480
[George] I'd like to live to see a black manager in baseball.

76
00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:13.960
[George] In 1997, on the 50th anniversary of his debut, Major League Baseball took the unprecedented step of retiring number 42 across all 30 Major League teams forever, ensuring that Jackie Robinson's courage and sacrifice will never be forgotten.

77
00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:27.400
[George] Following his retirement, Robinson dedicated his life to economic empowerment for black Americans, arguing that political civil rights were hollow without the financial muscle of capital, bank credit, and home ownership.

78
00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:42.120
[George] In his later years, plagued by failing eyesight and heart disease, Robinson never ceased speaking out, publicly criticizing Major League Baseball for failing to hire black managers, third base coaches, and front office executives.

79
00:13:42.120 --> 00:14:01.880
[George] When Commissioner Bud Selig permanently retired number 42 across all Major League Ballparks on April 15, 1997, it was an unprecedented tribute to a man whose courage, athletic brilliance, and moral discipline had forced America to live up to its founding promise of equal liberty for all.

80
00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:05.400
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

81
00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:13.320
[Rebecca] Jackie Robinson's athletic mastery, moral restraint, and heroic bravery broke barriers far beyond the baseball diamond.

82
00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:21.360
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the thrilling and defiant life of Mohammed Ali, the Louisville lip, and the greatest of all time.

83
00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:24.480
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

